views {

  view index {
    title 'System context'
    description '''
      **A local, single-user tool that sorts a shared inbox with a language model.**

      Click any box to go inside it. Every box carries a plain-English explanation and a link to the
      design section or file it came from.

      ### What this map is
      This started as a design model written *before* the code existed. **It is now the as-built map**:
      every element has graduated to a real file, so each box carries both a `spec` anchor into the design
      section that decided it and a `file` anchor into the code that implements it. Click a box, then its
      **source** link.

      Exactly two boxes are still dashed and muted, and that is deliberate — an Airtable store and a Slack
      notifier are **not built by choice**. Each sits behind an interface that has a real working
      implementation, which is what makes them seams rather than gaps.

      ### What surprised me
      The supplied sample data file is *itself* one of the planted edge cases — not just the messages inside
      it. It contains escape sequences that are illegal in JSON, so reading it with a strict parser fails on
      the whole file. Verified rather than assumed: the parse fails at line 98, and repairing the escapes
      recovers all thirteen messages with the corrupted bytes still intact, so a later stage can still
      recognise one message as garbage instead of quietly cleaning it up.

      The repair itself hid a second trap. A naive global replace of `\\xHH` also matches the `\\x` inside a
      *legitimately* escaped backslash, silently rewriting that value. The loader walks the string instead.

      ### Coverage, honestly
      Sixteen design decisions are recorded in `docs/DESIGN.md` section 9. 232 tests pass across 13 files,
      with no API key configured — every test injects its network function rather than calling out. Three
      real defects were found by tests written against this design and then fixed: a discourse marker read
      as a complaint, an undeclared field accepted instead of rejected, and a chain escalation that
      re-requested one model five times because it recorded the slug the provider *answered* with rather
      than the one we asked for.

      Verified end to end against a live provider: the queue of thirteen triaged in 5.1 seconds for
      $0.0022 across twelve model calls, and one schema-invalid response recovered on the retry path
      without being asked to.

      The number that argues against the design: measured cold on sixteen held-out messages the
      deterministic layer gets **1** right, against 6 of 6 on the supplied thirteen. It is a
      high-precision net rather than a general classifier, and the model carries unseen mail.
      `eval/README.md` has the table. Nothing here was inferred from a framework convention.

      ### What to read next
      The **App layers** view, for how the browser and the server process divide the work — and where the
      API key is and is not.
    '''
    include *
    style operator {
      color muted
    }
  }

  view layers of triage {
    title 'App layers — and the key boundary'
    description '''
      **The claim this view exists to make visible: the API key lives in exactly one box, and nothing
      that crosses to the browser carries it.**

      The browser holds no credential of any kind — not a key, not a token, not a masked placeholder. It
      asks the local server for rows. The server process reads the key from the environment with an empty
      variable prefix, which is the specific line that keeps it out of the browser bundle, and never
      returns it in any response.

      ### Why there is no offline mode
      A key-less demo mode was designed and then cut. It would have been a cushion for a stated
      requirement — the tool calls a model per message, so with no key it refuses to start and names the
      two commands that fix it. Failing fast with a legible message is the behaviour; degrading quietly
      is not.

      ### What surprised me
      The database and the credential want the same home. Because the store needs a server process
      anyway, the key has somewhere natural to live and no separate service is needed to hold it — which
      is what made a hosted key-relay unnecessary rather than forbidden.

      ### What to read next
      **Engine internals**, where the two verdicts are produced and combined.
    '''
    include *
    include triage.browser.*
    include triage.node.*
    include operator, openrouter, fixture, taxonomy

    style triage.browser {
      color green
    }
    style triage.node {
      color amber
    }
    style element.tag = #seam {
      color muted
    }
  }

  view engine of triage.node.engine {
    title 'Engine internals — one model call, two verdicts'
    description '''
      **Two claims here, and they are the load-bearing decisions of the whole design.**

      ### One model call per message
      Reconciliation — the step that combines the deterministic verdict with the model's — is ordinary
      code. It calls no model. There is exactly one outbound call per message and it has already
      happened by the time reconciliation runs. A test pins this by injecting a network function that
      throws if touched.

      ### The rule layer briefs the model but does not vote in front of it
      Follow the two arrows out of the rule floor. One goes to the prompt carrying **observations** —
      factual readings like *this text contains a first-person claim of being a client* or *this text
      contains a deadline expression* — reported symmetrically, every signal present or absent, in a
      fixed order with no emphasis. The other goes to reconciliation carrying the **verdict**.

      The verdict never reaches the prompt. Handing a model a suggested answer is absorbed silently: the
      reply still reads as independent reasoning while no longer being any, and the comparison downstream
      would then be two opinions tracing to one source. Agreement would rise, which reads as validation.

      ### What surprised me
      The output field order turned out to matter. Text is generated in sequence, so the schema emits the
      summary and the supporting evidence *before* the category and priority — putting the labels first
      would decide the answer before the reasoning that justifies it.

      ### What to read next
      Any of the three traces, which play step by step: the ordinary path, the unreadable body, and
      the failure that queues itself.
    '''
    include *
    include -> triage.node.engine
    include openrouter, triage.node.store

    style triage.node.engine.reconcile {
      color indigo
    }
    style triage.node.engine.rules {
      color green
    }
  }

  view data of triage.node.store {
    title 'Data — two tables, and the seam'
    description '''
      **Two tables, not three.** A message with nothing readable in it is an ordinary result row carrying
      the label `junk` and a `reason`, so there is no separate holding area for it — which removed a surface
      from the interface. An earlier design parked those messages in their own bucket; folding them into
      the queue as just another tag is what let the grid be a single list.

      Result rows are keyed by message and carry a content fingerprint. That is what makes a re-run
      idempotent: an unchanged message is skipped, an edited one is triaged again.

      ### Two fields that are deliberately independent
      `category` says what the message *is*. `status` says whether we *finished*. Keeping them apart is
      what stops a failed call having to masquerade as a category — a failed call asserts no verdict at
      all, and the row shows the failure kind and attempt count instead of a plausible-looking label.

      ### The seam
      The Airtable store is **deliberately not built** and shown muted. The interface above it is real
      and has a working implementation behind it, so this is a named migration path rather than an
      abstraction invented for a document.
    '''
    include *
    include triage.node.db
    include -> triage.node.store

    style element.tag = #seam {
      color muted
    }
  }
}
